@node What is Niori
@section What is Niori

@cindex medical records
Niori is a personal medical information manager for GNU Emacs.  It
keeps your @dfn{medications}, @dfn{visits}, @dfn{lab tests} and
@dfn{vaccinations} in a single local SQLite database, presented
through a dashboard that surfaces what needs attention: upcoming
appointments, abnormal results and boosters that are due.  The reports
and scans behind those records (PDF, DOCX, images) live in the same
data directory, attached to the record they belong to.

Rather than reimplementing UI toolkits, it composes the
platform Emacs already provides: @code{tabulated-list-mode} rosters,
@code{widget} forms, the built-in @code{sqlite} binding, Modus themes
and transient menus.

It runs as a package inside your Emacs, or as a standalone application
on a stock, configuration-free Emacs (@pxref{Installing as a standalone
application}).  Your data stays yours: one data directory holds the
database and every attached document, offline by default, with an
optional GPG-encrypted Org export and automatic revision snapshots you
can diff with @code{ediff} (@pxref{History and revisions}).  Niori is
free software under GPL-3.0-or-later, uses lexical binding everywhere
and depends on GNU ELPA packages only.  This manual describes version
0.1.0 of niori.el.

If Emacs is new to you, start with @ref{Introduction to the Emacs
way} (Appendix A): it explains how the key bindings in this manual
are written, the words Emacs uses for everyday things, and what GPG
and EasyPG are.  Everyone else can skip it.  For more about Emacs
itself, see @uref{https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/, the GNU Emacs
web site}.

@node Features
@section Features

@cindex modules
Niori tracks four kinds of clinical records.  Each is an independent
module with its own roster, form and dashboard section:

@multitable @columnfractions 0.20 0.45 0.35
@headitem Module @tab What you track @tab Dashboard section
@item Medications @tab name, dosage, form, dates, prescriber, instructions @tab active medications list
@item Visits @tab date, doctor, facility, reason, diagnosis, notes @tab upcoming visits (next 30 days)
@item Lab tests @tab date, name, result, unit, reference range, flag @tab latest result per test, abnormal flags
@item Vaccinations @tab date, vaccine, dose, route, lot, provider, next due @tab boosters due / overdue
@end multitable

@noindent
Several features cut across all modules:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@dfn{Rosters} (sortable @code{tabulated-list-mode} buffers), @dfn{forms}
(validated @code{widget} buffers with date checks) and @dfn{detail
views} with buttons.
@item
@dfn{Documents}: attach any file (PDF, DOCX, image, @dots{}) to a
record; it is copied into the data directory and opened with
@code{doc-view}, @code{image-mode} or an external viewer
(@pxref{Documents}).
@item
@dfn{Letters} composed from visit records, with @code{flyspell}
spell-checking and optional @code{yasnippet} templates
(@pxref{Visits and letters}).
@item
@dfn{Exports}: Org (optionally encrypted), CSV and FHIR JSON
(@pxref{Exporting data}).
@item
@dfn{History}: every change or deletion is snapshotted, and any record
can be diffed against its previous revision (@pxref{History and
revisions}).
@item
@dfn{Backups}: one keystroke copies the database to a timestamped file
(@pxref{Backups}).
@item
FHIR R4 export plus an authenticated FHIR client
(@pxref{FHIR interoperability}).
@end itemize

@node Requirements
@section Requirements

@cindex requirements
Niori needs GNU Emacs 29.1 or later, built with SQLite support.  That
is the default on major distributions; Niori checks at startup and
tells you if SQLite is missing (@pxref{No SQLite support}).

The following tools are optional; the features they support degrade
gracefully without them:

@table @asis
@item @command{gpg}
Needed for the encrypted Org export (@pxref{Encryption}).
@item @command{hunspell} or @command{aspell}
Needed for spell-checking letters (@pxref{Visits and letters}).
@item A system opener
@command{xdg-open} on GNU/Linux, @command{open} on macOS, the shell's
own associations on MS-Windows, for documents Emacs cannot display
(DOCX, XLSX, @dots{}) (@pxref{Documents}).
@item A FHIR server
Only if you want to retrieve records from one
(@pxref{FHIR interoperability}).
@end table

@noindent
The GNU ELPA dependencies @code{compat}, @code{transient},
@code{auto-dark} and @code{fontaine} are installed automatically when
you install the package (@pxref{Installing as a package}).

@node Concepts
@section Concepts

@cindex concepts
These terms recur throughout the manual:

@table @dfn
@item Module
A clinical domain (medications, visits, lab tests, vaccinations).  A
module registers its database schema, roster columns, form fields and
dashboard section; a generic view layer renders all of it, so a module
file contains no buffer code of its own.
@item Roster
A sortable list buffer showing all records of one kind
(@pxref{Rosters}).
@item Form
A @code{widget} buffer for creating or editing one record, with
validated fields and date checks (@pxref{Forms}).
@item Detail view
A read-only buffer describing one record, with buttons for the actions
that apply to it (@pxref{Detail views}).
@item Dashboard
The central buffer combining each module's section into one overview of
what needs attention (@pxref{The dashboard}).
@item Data directory
The single directory holding the SQLite database, attached documents,
backups and exports; the unit of backup, sync and relocation
(@pxref{The data directory}).
@item Revision
A JSON snapshot of the previous state of a record, written
automatically on every update or delete (@pxref{Revisions and audit
trail}).
@end table

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

This chapter describes how to obtain, install and run Niori.

@menu
* Installing as a package::
* Installing as a standalone application::
* First run::
* Upgrading::
@end menu

@node Installing as a package
@section Installing as a package

@cindex installation, as a package
The package is called @code{niori} (this repository is
@uref{https://github.com/ianikeev/niori.el, niori.el}).  Once it is on
MELPA, install it with @code{use-package}:

@lisp
(use-package niori
  :ensure t
  :commands (niori niori-dashboard))
@end lisp

@noindent
To install it straight from a checkout while it is not yet packaged,
add the repository to your load path:

@lisp
(use-package niori
  :load-path "~/src/niori.el"
  :commands (niori niori-dashboard))
@end lisp

@noindent
The GNU ELPA dependencies (@code{compat}, @code{transient},
@code{auto-dark}, @code{fontaine}) are declared in @file{niori.el} and
installed automatically; @code{package.el} users who prefer
@code{use-package} need nothing else.  A MELPA recipe ships in
@file{recipes/niori}.

@node Installing as a standalone application
@section Installing as a standalone application

@cindex installation, standalone
@cindex standalone application
Niori can run as a full application on a stock, configuration-free
Emacs (@code{emacs -Q}; @pxref{Running without an init file}):

@example
git clone https://github.com/ianikeev/niori.el
./niori.el/bin/niori
@end example

@noindent
That is all.  On the first run (when online) the launcher installs the
GNU ELPA dependencies plus @code{vertico}, @code{consult},
@code{orderless} and @code{which-key}; run fully offline it degrades
gracefully to built-ins (Modus themes with day/night switching,
@code{fido-vertical-mode}, plain keymap help).  Set the environment
variable @env{NIORI_NO_NET} to @code{1} to always start offline
(@pxref{Environment variables}).

To install it properly, with a desktop entry:

@example
make install              # defaults to ~/.local
# or: make install PREFIX=/usr/local
@end example

@noindent
This installs @file{bin/niori}, the Elisp sources under
@file{share/emacs/site-lisp/niori/}, the icon, and
@file{etc/niori.desktop} rewritten with the absolute @code{Exec=} path.

@node First run
@section First run

@cindex first run
The first time Niori opens its database, it creates the data directory
(owner-only, mode @code{0700}, with the @file{.niori-data} layout
marker) and the SQLite file inside it, then runs any pending migrations
(@pxref{Schema migrations}).  The database starts empty: there is no
seed data.  Open the dashboard with @kbd{M-x niori} and add your first
record (@pxref{Starting out}).  If Emacs is new to you, read the
survival kit in @ref{A survival kit} first.

@node Upgrading
@section Upgrading

@cindex upgrading
Schema changes run through numbered migrations, recorded in the
@code{schema_migrations} table.  A new version upgrades your database
in place: you install it and keep using the same data directory.  New
@emph{tables}---like the @code{attachments} table of a later
release---appear on the next open without a migration.

