@node Customizing Niori
@section Customizing Niori

@cindex customization
All user options live in the @code{niori} customization group: type
@kbd{M-x customize-group RET niori RET} to browse them with
descriptions and types, or set them with @code{setq} in your init
file.  There is no configuration file of Niori's own to edit.  The
standalone application runs without an init file; it is configured
through its built-in defaults and the environment variables
(@pxref{Environment variables}).

When Niori runs inside your Emacs it never touches your theme or your
fonts; the appearance layer only activates for the standalone
application (@pxref{Appearance}).

@node Variable reference
@section Variable reference

@cindex variables
@cindex user options
The essentials, all in the @code{niori} group:

@table @code
@item niori-data-directory
@vindex niori-data-directory
The data directory: database, documents, backups, exports.  Default
@file{~/.local/share/niori}, subject to the environment
(@pxref{Environment variables}).
@item niori-data-file
@vindex niori-data-file
Default @code{nil}, meaning the database lives inside the data
directory.  An absolute path here puts the database elsewhere.
@item niori-documents-directory
@vindex niori-documents-directory
Default @code{nil}, meaning attached documents live inside the data
directory.  An absolute path here puts them elsewhere.
@item niori-attachments-import-method
@vindex niori-attachments-import-method
How an attached file enters the data directory: @code{copy} (default),
@code{move} or @code{symlink}.
@item niori-attachments-external-opener
@vindex niori-attachments-external-opener
External viewer for formats Emacs cannot display: @command{xdg-open} on
GNU/Linux, @command{open} on macOS; MS-Windows uses
@code{w32-shell-execute}.
@item niori-store-tighten-data-directory
@vindex niori-store-tighten-data-directory
What happens on open when other users can reach the data directory:
@code{t} (default) tightens it to owner-only, @code{advise} keeps your
modes but reports once per session, @code{nil} stays silent
(@pxref{File permissions}).
@item niori-store-keep-revisions
@vindex niori-store-keep-revisions
Keep the revision audit trail; default @code{t} (@pxref{Revisions and
audit trail}).
@item niori-visits-upcoming-days
@vindex niori-visits-upcoming-days
Dashboard window for upcoming visits; default 30.
@item niori-vaccinations-due-days
@vindex niori-vaccinations-due-days
Dashboard window for boosters due; default 90.
@item niori-letter-flyspell
@vindex niori-letter-flyspell
Whether letters are spell-checked; default @code{auto}, which enables
checking when a spell-checking binary exists (@pxref{Visits and
letters}).
@item niori-encryption-recipients
@vindex niori-encryption-recipients
GPG keys for encrypted exports; default @code{nil}
(@pxref{Encryption}).
@item niori-fhir-server-url
@vindex niori-fhir-server-url
Base URL of a FHIR server for retrieval; default @code{nil}
(@pxref{FHIR interoperability}).
@item niori-appearance-manage
@vindex niori-appearance-manage
Whether Niori manages theme and font; default @code{nil}, @code{t} in
the standalone application (@pxref{Appearance}).
@item niori-appearance-font-family
@vindex niori-appearance-font-family
Monospaced font used when the appearance layer manages fonts;
auto-detected by default.
@item niori-standalone-cua
@vindex niori-standalone-cua
Set to @code{t} to give standalone users the usual CUA cut/copy/paste
bindings (@pxref{Starting out}).
@end table

@node Environment variables
@section Environment variables

@cindex environment variables
Two environment variables matter most:

@table @env
@item NIORI_DATA_DIR
Overrides @code{niori-data-directory}.  Useful for the standalone
application, which has no init file to customize.
@item NIORI_NO_NET
Set to @code{1} to make the standalone bootstrap skip all package
installation and always start offline.
@end table

@noindent
The data directory itself resolves in this order: @env{$NIORI_DATA_DIR}
when set; otherwise @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/niori} (usually
@file{~/.local/share/niori}); on MS-Windows, @file{%LOCALAPPDATA%\niori}.

@node Appearance
@section Appearance

@cindex appearance
@cindex themes
Inside your Emacs, Niori never touches your theme or your fonts:
@code{niori-appearance-manage} is @code{nil} by default and the
appearance layer stays out of the way.  It only activates for the
standalone application, which presents a complete, coherent look on its
own.  There it prefers the GNU ELPA @code{auto-dark} package, switching
between the @code{modus-operandi} and @code{modus-vivendi} themes with
the day/night cycle, and @code{fontaine} for font presets, with
built-in fallbacks when those packages are not available.  The font
family is auto-detected; set @code{niori-appearance-font-family} to
override it.

@node Text size
@section Text size

@cindex text scale
@cindex font size
Once @code{niori-mode} is enabled (@kbd{M-x niori}, @pxref{Starting
out}), the application follows the usual desktop zoom keys: @kbd{C-=}
and @kbd{C-+} make the text larger, @kbd{C--} makes it smaller.  Repeat
a key to keep growing or shrinking.  The keys work in every buffer,
because @code{niori-mode} is a global minor mode; the scale itself is a
per-buffer setting, so a roster, the dashboard and a letter each keep
their own size.

@table @kbd
@item C-=
@itemx C-+
@kindex C-=
@kindex C-+
Increase the text size of the current buffer.
@item C--
@kindex C--
Decrease the text size of the current buffer.
@item C-x C-0 0
@kindex C-x C-0
Reset the text size of the current buffer to the default
(@code{text-scale-adjust}, built into Emacs).
@end table

@noindent
Note that @kbd{C--} shadows the usual negative prefix argument while
@code{niori-mode} is enabled; use @kbd{M--} when a command needs a
negative argument.

@node Optional integrations
@section Optional integrations

@cindex integrations
Niori prefers mature Emacs components over new code, and every optional
integration is guarded at runtime: absence is a graceful downgrade, not
an error.  When installed, the following light up automatically:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{transient}: the @kbd{C-c M ?} command menu, runtime-defined, with
a fallback when absent;
@item
@code{which-key}: keymap discoverability (built into Emacs 30, GNU ELPA
otherwise);
@item
@code{vertico}, @code{consult}, @code{orderless}: completion in the
standalone application, auto-installed when online, with
@code{fido-vertical-mode} as the fallback;
@item
@code{yasnippet}: letter templates from @file{snippets/};
@item
@code{csv-mode}: CSV exports open in it automatically;
@item
@code{org-ql}: structured queries over exported Org archives.
@end itemize

@noindent
MELPA-only packages are never hard requirements: the package header
lists GNU ELPA dependencies only (@pxref{Requirements}).

@node Data and Privacy
@chapter Data and Privacy

This chapter describes how Niori stores and handles information.

@menu
* The data directory::
* File permissions::
* Revisions and audit trail::
* Backups and relocation::
* Attachment safety::
* Encryption::
* Network access::
* Schema migrations::
@end menu

@node The data directory
@section The data directory

@cindex data directory
Everything Niori writes lives in one directory you choose---the unit of
backup, sync and relocation.  Its location resolves to
@env{$NIORI_DATA_DIR} when set, otherwise to @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/niori}
(usually @file{~/.local/share/niori}), or @file{%LOCALAPPDATA%\niori} on
MS-Windows (@pxref{Environment variables}):

@example
~/.local/share/niori/
|-- .niori-data          layout marker ("layout-version: 1")
|-- niori.db             the database
|-- documents/           attached files, one folder per record
|   `-- visits/42/2026-11-20-lab-report.pdf
|-- backups/             timestamped database copies
|-- exports/             Org / CSV / FHIR output
`-- letters/             reserved for saved letters
@end example

@noindent
The @code{attachments} table stores paths @emph{relative} to the
documents directory (@file{visits/42/2026-11-20-lab-report.pdf}), plus
title, size, MIME type and SHA-256 checksum (@pxref{Attachment
safety}).

@node File permissions
@section File permissions

@cindex permissions
@cindex file permissions
On POSIX systems everything Niori writes is owner-only: the directory
itself (mode @code{0700}, re-applied on every open---you are told when
an existing loose directory is tightened), the documents inside it, the
database, the backups and exports, and the @file{-journal},
@file{-wal} and @file{-shm} files SQLite keeps beside the database.
Those sidecar files are created while the process umask is narrowed, so
they are private from the instant they exist, instead of needing a
@command{chmod} that would come too late.

On MS-Windows none of that means anything: Emacs maps @code{chmod} to
the read-only attribute there, so Niori performs no mode changes at all
and leaves protection to the NTFS permissions of the directory---which
is why the default is the per-user @file{%LOCALAPPDATA%} folder rather
than @file{~/.local}.

If you share the directory on purpose,
@code{niori-store-tighten-data-directory} decides what happens:

@table @asis
@item @code{t} (default)
re-apply mode @code{0700} on open and say so in one line;
@item @code{advise}
keep your modes, report once per session and name the fix;
@item @code{nil}
keep your modes, say nothing.
@end table

@noindent
In the latter two cases, @kbd{M-x niori-store-secure-data-directory}
fixes the tree on demand, regardless of the policy.  It never follows
symbolic links, so a document imported with the opt-in @code{symlink}
method keeps the permissions of the file you own elsewhere.  A
deliberately shared folder---a family NAS, a group-readable backup
volume---can therefore stay shared without being silently overridden on
every open.

Write-ahead logging is deliberately not enabled: the data directory is
meant to be synced as a whole, and a live @file{-wal} file on a
Syncthing or network folder is a corruption source rather than a
performance win.

@node Revisions and audit trail
@section Revisions and audit trail

@cindex revisions
@cindex audit trail
Medical data is edited rarely, but mistakes matter.  Every update or
delete first snapshots the previous record, as JSON, into the
@code{revisions} table: a real audit trail inside the same database
file, at the cost of one JSON write per change.  The behaviour is
controlled by @code{niori-store-keep-revisions} (default @code{t}).

To inspect a change, @kbd{D} in a roster or @kbd{C-c M D} diffs the
record against its previous revision with @code{ediff}
(@pxref{History and revisions}).

@node Backups and relocation
@section Backups and relocation

@cindex relocation
@findex niori-store-switch-data-directory
@kbd{C-c M b} writes a timestamped copy of the database into
@file{backups/} (@pxref{Backups}); keep a copy on offline media.
Because attaching copies files by default, the data directory is a
complete copy of the record, and ``back up this folder'' is sufficient
advice.

Moving an installation is a directory move plus @kbd{M-x
niori-store-switch-data-directory}: the command closes the connection,
re-points @code{niori-data-directory}, reopens the database (running
migrations) and refreshes open buffers.  It asks before adopting a
folder that holds no Niori data yet, and keeps using the old directory
if the new one cannot be opened.

@node Attachment safety
@section Attachment safety

@cindex attachments, safety
Documents are treated with care:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Paths are stored relative to the documents directory, and expanding a
stored path refuses anything that would escape it, so a hand-edited row
cannot turn into an arbitrary file read.
@item
Attaching @emph{copies} the file by default (the original stays where
it was); @code{niori-attachments-import-method} allows @code{move} and
@code{symlink} for large scans, at the cost of no longer being
self-contained.
@item
Detaching asks before removing a file, and never removes one another
row still points at.
@item
Deleting a @emph{record} asks first and keeps the files.
@kbd{M-x niori-attachments-check} lists missing files and documents
orphaned by a deleted record; @kbd{M-x niori-attachments-purge-orphans}
cleans the orphans up.
@end itemize

@node Encryption
@section Encryption

@cindex encryption
The Org export can be encrypted with EasyPG: invoke it with a prefix
argument, @kbd{C-u C-c M E o}, and it is encrypted to the keys in
@code{niori-encryption-recipients}.  The @command{gpg} prerequisite is
listed in @ref{Requirements}; what GPG and EasyPG actually are is
explained in @ref{GPG and EasyPG}, written for readers meeting them
for the first time.

If you keep narrative notes in Org files instead, the built-in
@code{org-crypt} encrypts subtrees on save---Niori stays out of the way
of both styles.

@node Network access
@section Network access

@cindex privacy
@cindex network
Nothing leaves your machine unless you call the FHIR client.  There is
no telemetry and no sync service; the data directory is offline by
default.

For FHIR retrieval, set @code{niori-fhir-server-url} to the server's
base URL and keep the bearer token in @code{auth-source}---a line like
the following in @file{.authinfo}:

@example
machine fhir.example.com secret TOKEN
@end example

@noindent
Then @kbd{M-x niori-fhir-fetch} issues authenticated requests with the
built-in @code{url} library and returns parsed JSON
(@pxref{FHIR interoperability}).

@node Schema migrations
@section Schema migrations

@cindex schema migrations
Schema changes run through numbered migrations recorded in the
@code{schema_migrations} table.  A new version upgrades the database in
place; new tables appear on the next open without a migration
(@pxref{Upgrading}).  Table and column names come exclusively from the
schema registry, and values are always bound parameters, so the query
surface is injection-safe.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

This chapter covers common problems and their solutions.

@menu
* No SQLite support::
* Offline fallback::
* Permission warnings::
* Documents will not open::
* Spell-checking does not start::
* Encrypted export fails::
* FHIR retrieval fails::
* Orphaned attachments::
* Key binding conflicts::
* Restoring a database::
@end menu

@node No SQLite support
@section No SQLite support

Niori needs Emacs 29.1 or later built with SQLite support.  That is the
default on major distributions, and Niori checks at startup and tells
you if the binding is missing.  Install an Emacs build with SQLite
support (@pxref{Requirements}) and Niori will open its database.

@node Offline fallback
@section Offline fallback

If the standalone application starts with plain themes and simple
completion, it could not reach GNU ELPA.  This is deliberate: every
optional package is guarded, and the application must start on a bare,
offline Emacs.  The first run installs @code{vertico},
@code{consult}, @code{orderless} and @code{which-key} when online; set
@env{NIORI_NO_NET} to @code{1} to always start offline (@pxref{Environment
variables}).  On a degraded start you still get Modus themes with
day/night switching, @code{fido-vertical-mode} and plain keymap help.

@node Permission warnings
@section Permission warnings

If Niori reports a loose data directory on open, either let it tighten
the directory (the default, @code{niori-store-tighten-data-directory}
@code{t}), or, if you share the directory on purpose, set the option to
@code{advise} or @code{nil} and run @kbd{M-x
niori-store-secure-data-directory} whenever you want it fixed
(@pxref{File permissions}).

@node Documents will not open
@section Documents will not open

PDFs open with @code{doc-view} and images with @code{image-mode}; other
formats (DOCX, XLSX, @dots{}) go to the external opener: the program in
@code{niori-attachments-external-opener}, which defaults to
@command{xdg-open} on GNU/Linux, @command{open} on macOS, and
@code{w32-shell-execute} on MS-Windows.  If nothing happens, check that
the opener matches your system (@pxref{Variable reference}).

@node Spell-checking does not start
@section Spell-checking does not start

Letters are spell-checked with @code{flyspell} only when a
@command{hunspell} or @command{aspell} binary exists;
@code{niori-letter-flyspell} defaults to @code{auto}, which enables
checking only in that case (@pxref{Visits and letters}).  Install
either binary to get spell-checking.

@node Encrypted export fails
@section Encrypted export fails

Encrypted Org export needs two things: @command{gpg} installed, and at
least one key in @code{niori-encryption-recipients}.  Remember that
encryption is requested with a prefix argument (@kbd{C-u C-c M E o});
without it, the export is written in plain Org (@pxref{Encryption}).

@node FHIR retrieval fails
@section FHIR retrieval fails

@kbd{M-x niori-fhir-fetch} needs @code{niori-fhir-server-url} and a
bearer token in @code{auth-source} for the server's host: a
@file{.authinfo} line such as @code{machine fhir.example.com secret
TOKEN} (@pxref{Network access}).

@node Orphaned attachments
@section Orphaned attachments

Deleting a record keeps its files on purpose (@pxref{Attachment
safety}), so documents can outlive their record.  @kbd{M-x
niori-attachments-check} lists missing files and orphans; @kbd{M-x
niori-attachments-purge-orphans} removes the orphans.  Files still
referenced by another record are never removed.

@node Key binding conflicts
@section Key binding conflicts

The @kbd{C-c M} prefix was chosen to avoid collisions with other
packages that use popular prefixes (for instance @kbd{C-c n}, used by
deft).  @code{niori-mode} is a global minor mode with zero footprint
until you run @kbd{M-x niori}.  If another package shadows one of the
bindings, @kbd{C-c M C-h} shows the full keymap overview
(@pxref{Starting out}).

@node Restoring a database
@section Restoring a database

@kbd{C-c M b} writes timestamped copies into @file{backups/}
(@pxref{Backups}).  To restore one, close the database first with
@kbd{M-x niori-store-close}, copy the backup over @file{niori.db} in
the data directory, and start Niori again.

@node Introduction to the Emacs way
@appendix Introduction to the Emacs Way

@cindex Emacs, introduction
This appendix is for readers who have never used Emacs---perhaps you
are coming to Niori as a standalone application, on a computer where
Emacs was installed mainly to run it (@pxref{Installing as a standalone
application}).  It explains how the key bindings in this manual are
written, the small set of keys that gets you out of trouble, the words
Emacs uses for everyday things, and what GPG and EasyPG are.  If you
have used Emacs before, skip this appendix.

@menu
* How key bindings are written::
* A survival kit::
* Words Emacs uses::
* GPG and EasyPG::
* Running without an init file::
@end menu

@node How key bindings are written
@section How key bindings are written

@cindex key notation
@cindex Control key
@cindex Meta key
Commands in this manual are written as key sequences.  @kbd{C-} means
the @dfn{Control} key: @kbd{C-x} is ``hold Control and type
x''.  @kbd{M-} means @dfn{Meta}: on most keyboards that is the Alt
key, and on keyboards without one, pressing and releasing Esc before
the key works as a substitute.  @kbd{M-x} is therefore ``hold Alt and
type x''.

A sequence of several elements is typed in order, one after another.
@kbd{C-c M b}, Niori's backup command, means: hold Control and type
c, release everything, then hold Alt and type b.  Within one element
the keys are held together; between elements they are not.

Letters matter exactly as written: @kbd{C-a} and @kbd{C-A} are
different bindings, and @kbd{C-A} means Control with a @emph{shifted}
A.  A few keys have names instead of symbols: @key{RET} is the Enter
key, @key{SPC} the space bar, @key{TAB} the Tab key.  Where this
manual writes @kbd{M-x customize-group RET niori RET}, you type
@kbd{M-x}, the word @code{customize-group}, press @key{RET}, type
@code{niori} and press @key{RET} again.

@cindex running commands by name
@cindex prefix argument
@kbd{M-x} deserves a second look, because every Niori command can be
reached through it: @kbd{M-x} asks for a command name in the
minibuffer (@pxref{Words Emacs uses}), and @key{RET} runs it.  A
prefix argument modifies the command that follows: @kbd{C-u C-c M E o}
asks the Org export to encrypt itself (@pxref{Exporting data}).

@cindex help, built-in
Emacs documents itself.  @kbd{C-h k} asks ``what does this key do?'',
@kbd{C-h m} describes the current buffer and lists every key binding
active in it, and @kbd{C-h t} starts the built-in tutorial.  Inside
Niori, @kbd{C-c M C-h} shows all Niori bindings at once
(@pxref{Starting out}).

@node A survival kit
@section A survival kit

@cindex survival commands
Ten keys answer almost every first-day question.  None of them are
Niori's; they belong to Emacs everywhere.

@table @kbd
@item C-g
@cindex cancel a command
Quit whatever is happening: a half-typed key sequence, a minibuffer
prompt, a search, a hung command.  When in doubt, type @kbd{C-g}.
@item C-x C-c
Leave Emacs.
@item C-x C-f
@cindex open a file
Open a file: Emacs asks for its name in the minibuffer.  Use this to
look at an exported Org or CSV file.
@item C-x C-s
Save the current buffer to its file.
@item C-/
@cindex undo
Undo the last change (@kbd{C-x u} also works).
@item C-s
@cindex search
Search forward in the buffer; @kbd{C-r} searches backwards.  @key{RET}
stops the search where you are.
@item C-v
Scroll down a screenful; @kbd{M-v} scrolls up.  The @key{PgDn} and
@key{PgUp} keys do the same.
@item M-x
Run any command by name (@pxref{How key bindings are written}).  Every
@code{niori-} command mentioned in this manual works here, including
those without a key binding.
@item C-h k
Ask what a key does: type the key after @kbd{C-h k} and Emacs shows
its documentation.
@item C-h t
The Emacs tutorial: half an hour that repays itself for the rest of
the week.
@end table

@node Words Emacs uses
@section Words Emacs uses

@cindex Emacs vocabulary
Emacs calls everyday things by names of its own.  These recur
throughout the manual:

@table @dfn
@item Buffer
The content you edit or read---a file, a roster, a form, the
dashboard.  Niori's rosters, forms and detail views are buffers.
@item Window
A pane showing one buffer.  Emacs's @dfn{frame} is what your desktop
calls a window; a @dfn{window} in Emacs is one pane of it, and Emacs
shows several buffers side by side by splitting the frame into
windows.
@item Point
The cursor.  Commands act at point: in a roster, @kbd{RET} opens the
record under point (@pxref{Rosters}).
@item Minibuffer
@cindex minibuffer
@cindex echo area
The prompt area at the bottom of the frame, where Emacs asks questions
and accepts command names after @kbd{M-x}.  @key{RET} confirms and
@kbd{C-g} cancels.  One-line messages appear next to it, in the
@dfn{echo area}.
@item Mode
The active behaviour of a buffer.  A @dfn{major mode} says what the
buffer is---a @code{tabulated-list-mode} roster, @code{doc-view} for a
PDF---and @dfn{minor modes} layer extra behaviour on top.
@code{niori-mode} is a @emph{global} minor mode, active in every
buffer, which is why @kbd{C-c M} works everywhere (@pxref{Starting
out}).
@item Keymap
@cindex keymap
The table that connects a key to a command; one connection is a
@dfn{key binding}.  Prefix keys like @kbd{C-c M} select a whole
sub-table: the @kbd{b} in @kbd{C-c M b} is looked up inside Niori's
own keymap.
@item Init file
@cindex init file
The personal startup script Emacs runs at launch
(@file{~/.emacs.d/init.el} or @file{~/.emacs}), where @code{setq} and
@code{use-package} lines go.  The standalone application runs
@emph{without} one (@pxref{Running without an init file}).
@item Customize
The built-in settings browser: @kbd{M-x customize-group RET niori RET}
lists every Niori option with descriptions and types
(@pxref{Customizing Niori}).
@item Package
@cindex packages
@cindex GNU ELPA
@cindex MELPA
An installable extension.  GNU ELPA is the GNU archive Emacs installs
from by default; MELPA is a popular community archive.  Niori depends
on GNU ELPA packages only (@pxref{Requirements}).
@item Completion
@cindex completion
While you type in the minibuffer, Emacs offers matching choices and
@key{RET} picks one.  The standalone application installs
@code{vertico}, @code{consult} and @code{orderless} for this
(@pxref{Optional integrations}).
@end table

@node GPG and EasyPG
@section GPG and EasyPG

@cindex GPG
@cindex EasyPG
@cindex key pair
@cindex passphrase
@cindex gpg-agent
Two names appear wherever Niori encrypts something (@pxref{Encryption}).

@dfn{GPG}, the GNU Privacy Guard, is the free OpenPGP implementation:
a command-line program, @command{gpg}, that encrypts data to key
pairs.  A @dfn{key pair} has a public half, which you may publish and
which others encrypt @emph{to} you, and a secret half, protected by a
@dfn{passphrase}, which decrypts.  You generate a pair with
@command{gpg --full-generate-key} and list the ones you have with
@command{gpg --list-keys}; each is identified by a name and an e-mail
address.  The @code{niori-encryption-recipients} option holds such
identifiers: an encrypted export is encrypted to those public keys, so
only someone holding the matching secret keys---you, or a person you
chose---can read it.  A small helper program, @command{gpg-agent},
remembers the passphrase for a while, so you need not retype it.

@dfn{EasyPG} is the Emacs side of the same machinery, built into
Emacs.  The EasyPG Assistant manages keys interactively (@kbd{M-x
epa-list-keys}), and @code{epa-file} makes Emacs treat any file whose
name ends in @file{.gpg} transparently: it decrypts on open,
re-encrypts on save, and asks for the passphrase in the minibuffer
when @command{gpg-agent} does not still have it.  Niori's encrypted
export simply hands the file to this layer: open the export in Emacs
and you are reading it; @command{gpg --decrypt} does the same outside
Emacs.

@node Running without an init file
@section Running without an init file

@cindex init file, skipping
@cindex emacs -Q
Emacs normally runs your init file at startup: themes, fonts, key
tweaks and installed packages---everything you have put there.  The
@code{-Q} flag, short for ``quick'', starts Emacs without it: no
personal init file, no site-wide one, no X resources.  You get a
pristine, stock editor session that behaves the same on every machine
and every run.

That is exactly how Niori's standalone launcher works
(@pxref{Installing as a standalone application}): @command{bin/niori}
starts such a stock Emacs, loads Niori into it and lets you open the
dashboard with @kbd{M-x niori}, so the application never
inherits---and never touches---any personal configuration.  Two
practical consequences:

@itemize @bullet
@item
What persists between runs is your data---the data directory
(@pxref{The data directory})---not settings.  To point the standalone
application at a particular data directory, or to keep it offline, set
the environment variables @env{NIORI_DATA_DIR} and @env{NIORI_NO_NET}
(@pxref{Environment variables}); there is no init file to set options
in.
@item
Options you change interactively---@kbd{M-x customize-group RET niori
RET}, @kbd{M-x cua-mode} for cut/copy/paste keys (@pxref{Starting
out})---last for the session only.  If you later move Niori into your
own Emacs, the same settings belong in the init file instead
(@pxref{Customizing Niori}).
@end itemize

@noindent
If a first contact with Emacs itself feels rough, @kbd{C-h t} (the
tutorial) and the survival kit above are the shortest way in.

